// New articles, 17 November 2026 (part 4): plants, technology and space.
ADDART({id:"tulips",t:"Tulips",c:"Plants",i:"🌷",w:"Tulip",r:["flower","netherlandscountry","ottoman","stockm","turkeycountry"],
p:[["Spring bulbs","Tulips are spring flowers that grow from bulbs."],["From Central Asia","Wild tulips come from the mountains of Central Asia and Turkey."],["A famous mania","In 1637, Dutch tulip prices soared and then collapsed."]],
l:"Tulips are flowering plants of the genus Tulipa, in the lily family, that grow from bulbs and bloom in spring. There are about 75 to 100 wild species, native to a band stretching from southern Europe and North Africa across Turkey and Iran to the mountains of Central Asia, where the greatest variety grows. Each plant usually has a single cup-shaped flower with six petal-like tepals, in almost every color except true blue. Tulips were grown in Persia and became beloved in the Ottoman Empire, where an early 18th-century period of luxury is called the Tulip Era. They reached Western Europe in the 16th century, and the Netherlands became their center. In the Dutch ‘tulip mania’ of the 1630s, rare bulbs sold for more than the price of a house before prices crashed in February 1637. Today the Netherlands grows most of the world’s tulip bulbs, billions a year, and its Keukenhof gardens draw visitors from around the world.",
s:[["How tulips grow","A tulip bulb is an underground bud wrapped in fleshy scale leaves that store food. Bulbs need a cold winter before they will flower. After blooming, the leaves make food that is stored in new bulbs for the next year. Gardeners plant bulbs in autumn for flowers in spring."],["Tulips in the Ottoman Empire","Ottoman sultans filled their gardens with tulips, prized long slender flowers and painted them on tiles, textiles and pottery. The reign of Sultan Ahmed III, from 1718 to 1730, is called the Tulip Era for its gardens, festivals and Western-influenced culture."],["Tulip mania","Botanist Carolus Clusius planted tulips in Leiden around 1594, and they became a status symbol in the Dutch Republic. In the 1630s, people traded contracts for bulbs, and prices for rare striped varieties such as ‘Semper Augustus’ climbed wildly until the market collapsed in February 1637. Historians debate how much real harm the crash caused, but it became a famous example of a speculative bubble."],["Broken tulips","The most prized tulips in the 1630s had feathered, flame-like stripes. Scientists discovered in the 20th century that these were caused by a virus, spread by aphids, that weakened the bulbs. Modern striped tulips are bred to have stable patterns without the virus."]],
k:[["Genus","Tulipa (lily family)"],["Wild species","About 75–100"],["Native range","Southern Europe to Central Asia"],["Biggest grower","The Netherlands"],["Tulip mania crash","February 1637"]],
d:[["c. 1594","Clusius plants tulips in Leiden"],["1637","Tulip mania collapses"],["1718–1730","The Tulip Era in the Ottoman Empire"],["1928","Scientists link striped ‘broken’ tulips to a virus"],["1950","Keukenhof gardens open to the public"],["1953","First Canadian Tulip Festival in Ottawa"]],
s2:[["A gift of thanks","During World War II, the Dutch royal family sheltered in Ottawa, Canada. In 1945 Princess Juliana sent 100,000 tulip bulbs to thank Canadians for helping liberate the Netherlands, and the Netherlands still sends bulbs each year. The gift inspired the Canadian Tulip Festival."],["Keukenhof","Keukenhof, near Lisse in the Netherlands, is one of the world’s largest flower gardens. About 7 million bulbs are planted each autumn for an eight-week spring season, attracting more than a million visitors."]],
sp:["The search for a black tulip","Breeders spent centuries trying to grow a black tulip, inspired partly by Alexandre Dumas’s 1850 novel The Black Tulip. Varieties such as ‘Queen of Night’ are a very deep purple, close to black."],
g:[["Bulb","An underground bud with food-storing leaves"],["Tepal","A petal-like part of a flower"],["Speculative bubble","When prices rise far above real value, then crash"],["Genus","A group of closely related species"]],
f:["Wild tulips come from the mountains of Central Asia.","Tulip bulbs need a cold winter to flower.","Striped ‘broken’ tulips were caused by a virus."],
fun:"Tulip petals are edible, and during the Dutch ‘Hunger Winter’ of 1944–45, starving people ate tulip bulbs to survive."});

ADDART({id:"bonsai",t:"Bonsai",c:"Plants",i:"🪴",w:"Bonsai",r:["japan","trees","urbanfarm","china","buddh"],
p:[["Trees in pots","Bonsai is the art of growing miniature trees in containers."],["From China to Japan","It developed from Chinese penjing and was refined in Japan."],["Living for centuries","Some bonsai trees are hundreds of years old."]],
l:"Bonsai, a Japanese word meaning ‘planted in a container’, is the art of growing small trees in pots and shaping them to look like full-sized trees in nature. Bonsai are not special dwarf species: almost any tree or shrub, such as pines, junipers, maples, elms and figs, can be kept small by careful pruning of branches and roots, wiring branches into shape and growing the tree in a shallow pot. The art developed from penjing, the Chinese practice of creating miniature landscapes in trays, which spread to Japan by about the 12th century. Japanese Buddhist monks and later wealthy collectors refined it into a distinct art form valuing simplicity, balance and the appearance of age. Bonsai spread worldwide in the 20th century after international exhibitions. Well-cared-for bonsai can live for centuries and are passed down through generations; a Japanese white pine that survived the 1945 atomic bombing of Hiroshima is displayed in Washington, D.C.",
s:[["How bonsai are made","Growers start from seeds, cuttings, nursery plants or trees collected from the wild. They prune branches and roots, pinch new growth, and wrap copper or aluminum wire around branches to bend them, removing it before it cuts into the bark. Trees are repotted every few years to trim roots and refresh soil."],["Styles","Classic styles include formal upright, with a straight trunk; informal upright; slanting; cascade, where the tree hangs below the pot like a tree on a cliff; and literati, with a tall, slender, bare trunk. Groups of trees can create a miniature forest."],["Care","Most bonsai trees must live outdoors, experiencing seasons like normal trees; only tropical species such as figs do well indoors. Because pots are shallow, bonsai need frequent watering, sometimes daily. They also need sunlight, fertilizer and protection from frost."],["Bonsai around the world","Bonsai became widely known outside Asia after exhibitions such as the 1970 Osaka World Expo. Japan’s Omiya Bonsai Village near Tokyo has famous nurseries and a bonsai museum. Clubs and exhibitions now exist in many countries."]],
k:[["Meaning","‘Planted in a container’"],["Origin","Chinese penjing"],["Common trees","Pines, junipers, maples, figs"],["Lifespan","Can be centuries"],["Famous survivor","Hiroshima white pine"]],
d:[["c. 700 AD","Chinese art of penjing depicted in tomb paintings"],["c. 1100s","Miniature trees spread to Japan"],["1625","The Yamaki white pine’s training begins (by tradition)"],["1970","Osaka World Expo shows bonsai to the world"],["1976","Japan gives 53 bonsai to the United States"]],
s2:[["The Hiroshima survivor","A Japanese white pine trained by the Yamaki family since at least 1625 survived the atomic bombing of Hiroshima in 1945, protected by a wall around their nursery. In 1976 it was among 53 bonsai given by Japan to the United States, and it is displayed at the National Bonsai and Penjing Museum in Washington."],["Patience and philosophy","Bonsai is often linked with Zen ideas of patience, simplicity and harmony with nature. Creating a mature bonsai takes many years, and growers say the tree is never truly finished."]],
sp:["Penjing and saikei","In Chinese penjing, trees are often combined with rocks, water and tiny figures to create whole landscapes. A related Japanese art, saikei, arranges several trees and stones in a tray to form a miniature scene."],
g:[["Pruning","Cutting branches or roots to shape a plant"],["Wiring","Wrapping wire around branches to bend them"],["Penjing","Chinese art of miniature landscapes"],["Cascade","A style where the tree hangs below the pot"]],
f:["Bonsai are normal trees kept small, not special dwarf species.","Most bonsai must live outdoors.","Some bonsai are hundreds of years old."],
fun:"Some bonsai trees sell for more than a million dollars, and a pine at a 1981 exhibition in Japan was reported to sell for about 1.3 million dollars."});

ADDART({id:"roses",t:"Roses",c:"Plants",i:"🌹",w:"Rose",r:["flower","weddings","urbanfarm","fruits","ukcountry"],
p:[["A favorite flower","Roses are woody shrubs with fragrant flowers, grown for thousands of years."],["Thousands of varieties","There are about 300 wild species and tens of thousands of garden varieties."],["Symbol of love","Roses carry meanings of love, beauty and politics."]],
l:"Roses are woody flowering shrubs and climbers of the genus Rosa, in the rose family, which also includes apples, cherries, strawberries and almonds. There are about 300 wild species, mostly native to Asia, with others in Europe, North America and northwest Africa, and tens of thousands of cultivated varieties. Fossils show roses existed at least 35 million years ago. Wild roses usually have five petals, while garden roses are bred for many petals, rich colors and scent. Their stems have sharp prickles, often called thorns, and after flowering many produce fruits called rose hips, rich in vitamin C. People have grown roses for thousands of years in China, Persia, Greece and Rome for beauty, perfume and medicine. Rose oil and rosewater are used in perfumes and foods. Roses symbolize love, beauty and secrecy, and are national flowers of the United States and England.",
s:[["Wild and garden roses","Old garden roses, grown before 1867, include gallica, damask and China roses. Roses from China, brought to Europe around 1800, could flower repeatedly through the season. Crossing them with European roses produced modern roses; ‘La France’, introduced in 1867, is considered the first hybrid tea, the classic long-stemmed florist’s rose."],["Perfume and food","Damask roses are grown in Bulgaria’s Rose Valley, Turkey and Iran for rose oil, also called attar of roses. It takes about 3,000–5,000 kilograms of petals to make one kilogram of oil. Rosewater flavors sweets in the Middle East and South Asia, and rose hips make tea and syrup."],["Roses in history","The Wars of the Roses (1455–1487) between the English royal houses of Lancaster and York were later named after their badges, the red and white roses. The Tudor rose combined both. Empress Josephine of France collected about 250 kinds of roses at her garden at Malmaison."],["Meanings","Red roses traditionally mean love, white roses purity, and yellow roses friendship. The Latin phrase sub rosa, ‘under the rose’, means in secret, from an old custom of hanging a rose over meetings. Roses are a symbol of socialist and labor parties in Europe."]],
k:[["Genus","Rosa (rose family)"],["Wild species","About 300"],["Oldest fossils","About 35 million years old"],["Fruit","Rose hip"],["National flower of","USA, England and others"]],
d:[["c. 500 BC","Roses grown in Chinese gardens"],["1455–1487","Wars of the Roses in England"],["c. 1800","Repeat-flowering China roses reach Europe"],["1867","‘La France’, the first hybrid tea rose"],["1945","The ‘Peace’ rose is named"],["1986","Rose becomes US national flower"]],
s2:[["The Peace rose","A French breeder, Francis Meilland, created a pale yellow rose with pink edges just before World War II and sent cuttings abroad to save it. In the United States it was named ‘Peace’ on 29 April 1945, and delegates at the founding of the United Nations later that year each received one. It became one of the best-selling roses ever."],["Thorns or prickles?","Botanists say roses have prickles, not thorns: prickles grow from the outer skin of the stem and snap off easily, while true thorns are modified branches. The prickles help climbing roses grip and protect them from animals."]],
sp:["The oldest rose","The ‘Thousand-year Rose’ climbing the wall of Hildesheim Cathedral in Germany is often said to be the oldest living rose. It survived the destruction of the cathedral in a 1945 air raid; its roots sprouted again from the rubble."],
g:[["Rose hip","The fruit of a rose"],["Hybrid tea","A popular type of modern garden rose"],["Attar","Fragrant oil made from flowers"],["Prickle","A sharp outgrowth from a stem’s skin"]],
f:["Apples and strawberries belong to the rose family.","Rose oil takes thousands of kilograms of petals per kilogram.","Roses have prickles, not true thorns."],
fun:"Rose hips contain more vitamin C than oranges, and during World War II, British children collected them to make syrup."});

ADDART({id:"plantsenses",t:"How Plants Sense and Communicate",c:"Plants",i:"🌱",w:"Plant perception (physiology)",r:["photo","leaves","roots","fungi","carnivplant","mimosa","plantdefenses","plantgrowth"],
p:[["Plants sense the world","Plants detect light, gravity, touch, water and chemicals."],["Chemical messages","Plants release chemicals that warn neighbors and attract helpers."],["No brain needed","Plants respond without nerves or brains, through cells and hormones."]],
l:"Although plants cannot move from place to place, they constantly sense and respond to their surroundings. Plants detect the direction, color and length of daylight, grow their roots downward and shoots upward by sensing gravity, bend toward light, close leaves when touched and track water and nutrients in the soil. They use chemical signals called hormones and electrical signals to send information between parts of the plant. Plants also communicate: when insects chew their leaves, many release scented chemicals that warn nearby plants to strengthen defenses and attract predators of the pests. Roots exchange chemical signals with soil microbes and fungi. Scientists have shown that Venus flytraps count touches and that some plants respond to the sound of chewing insects. Some researchers describe these abilities as plant ‘intelligence’, while others argue the term is misleading because plants have no brains or nerves, and claims about a ‘wood-wide web’ remain debated.",
s:[["Sensing light and gravity","Plants have light-sensing proteins that detect red, far-red and blue light, telling them when to sprout, flower or grow taller to escape shade. Charles Darwin and his son Francis showed in 1880 that seedlings sense light at their tips and bend toward it. Special cells in roots contain heavy starch grains that settle downward, helping the root sense gravity."],["Touch and movement","The sensitive plant Mimosa pudica folds its leaves within seconds when touched. Climbing plants coil tendrils around supports. A 2016 study found that the Venus flytrap counts touches on its trigger hairs: two touches snap the trap shut, and five start digestion."],["Talking with chemicals","When caterpillars attack a plant, it can release airborne chemicals that attract wasps that lay eggs in the caterpillars. Acacia trees and tomato plants raise their defenses when neighbors are damaged. A 2014 study showed that thale cress plants made more defensive chemicals after hearing recordings of caterpillars chewing."],["The ‘wood-wide web’","Many plants partner with fungi whose threads connect roots underground, trading sugars for nutrients. Some studies suggested trees share resources and warnings through these networks. A 2023 review found that much of the evidence was weaker than popular accounts claim, and scientists continue to investigate."]],
k:[["Senses","Light, gravity, touch, water, chemicals"],["Internal signals","Hormones and electrical signals"],["Fast mover","Mimosa pudica"],["Counting plant","Venus flytrap"],["Debated idea","The ‘wood-wide web’"]],
d:[["1880","Darwin publishes The Power of Movement in Plants"],["1920","Day length found to control flowering"],["1983","Studies suggest damaged trees warn neighbors"],["1997","Fungal networks found linking trees"],["2016","Venus flytraps shown to count touches"],["2023","Stressed plants found to make ultrasonic clicks"]],
s2:[["Plants that click","In 2023, Israeli scientists reported that tomato and tobacco plants make ultrasonic popping sounds, too high for humans to hear, when they are dry or cut. The sounds may come from air bubbles in their water tubes. Whether insects or other plants respond to the sounds is not yet known."],["Are plants intelligent?","Some scientists use the term ‘plant neurobiology’ to describe how plants process information. Many others reject it, saying that plants have no neurons and that their responses, though complex, are not the same as thinking. Both sides agree that plants are far more active and responsive than they seem."]],
sp:["The sensitive plant","Mimosa pudica folds its leaflets when touched by moving water out of cells at the base of each leaf. Experiments in 2014 showed that plants dropped repeatedly without harm stopped folding, suggesting they ‘learned’ to ignore the harmless disturbance, and remembered for weeks."],
g:[["Hormone","A chemical messenger in a living thing"],["Tropism","Growth toward or away from something, like light"],["Mycorrhiza","A partnership between plant roots and fungi"],["Ultrasonic","Sound too high-pitched for humans to hear"]],
f:["Venus flytraps count touches before closing.","Roots sense gravity using heavy starch grains.","Stressed tomato plants make ultrasonic clicks."],
fun:"Freshly cut grass smells the way it does because the damaged blades release chemicals as a distress signal."});

ADDART({id:"herbs",t:"Herbs",c:"Plants",i:"🌿",w:"Herb",r:["spice","medicinalplants","urbanfarm","photo","plantbased","dandelions","saffron","lavender"],
p:[["Useful leaves","Herbs are plants whose leaves, flowers or seeds are used in cooking, medicine and fragrance."],["Ancient remedies","Many modern medicines came from plants used as herbal remedies."],["Easy to grow","Herbs like basil, mint and parsley grow well in small gardens and pots."]],
l:"In cooking, herbs are plants whose fresh or dried leaves, and sometimes flowers, are used to add flavor, such as basil, parsley, mint, rosemary, thyme, oregano, coriander (cilantro), dill and sage; they differ from spices, which usually come from seeds, bark, roots or fruit. In botany, the word ‘herb’ also means any plant without a woody stem. Many cooking herbs belong to the mint family, which have square stems and aromatic oils, or the carrot family. Herbs have been used as medicine for thousands of years: the Greek physician Dioscorides described about 600 medicinal plants in the first century AD, and monasteries grew herb gardens in medieval Europe. Several important modern drugs came from plants, including aspirin, based on a chemical in willow bark, and the malaria drug artemisinin, from sweet wormwood. Herbal remedies can have real effects but also side effects and drug interactions, so scientists test them carefully.",
s:[["Cooking herbs","Many cuisines are identified by their herbs: basil in Italian dishes, coriander and lemongrass in Southeast Asian food, dill in Scandinavian cooking and mint in Middle Eastern dishes. Soft herbs such as basil and parsley are usually added at the end of cooking, while woody herbs such as rosemary and thyme can cook for longer."],["Herbal medicine","Traditional systems of medicine, including Chinese medicine and Ayurveda in India, use thousands of plants. Scientists study these traditions to find new drugs. Some herbal remedies, such as St John’s wort, can interfere with prescription medicines, so doctors advise telling them about any herbs people take."],["From willow to aspirin","Ancient Egyptians and Greeks used willow bark for pain and fever. In the 19th century, chemists isolated salicin from it, and in 1897 chemists at the Bayer company made acetylsalicylic acid, sold as aspirin from 1899. It remains one of the world’s most widely used medicines."],["Growing herbs","Many herbs come from the sunny Mediterranean and like well-drained soil. Basil needs warmth, while mint spreads quickly and is often grown in pots to stop it taking over gardens. Herbs can grow on windowsills, making them popular in small homes and urban farms."]],
k:[["Common herbs","Basil, mint, parsley, rosemary, thyme"],["Main plant families","Mint and carrot families"],["Herb vs spice","Herbs are usually leaves; spices are seeds, bark or roots"],["Plant-based drug","Aspirin (from willow bark)"],["Nobel-winning herb drug","Artemisinin (2015)"]],
d:[["c. 1550 BC","Egyptian Ebers Papyrus lists hundreds of herbal remedies"],["c. 60 AD","Dioscorides writes De Materia Medica"],["1545","First university botanical garden founded in Padua"],["1673","Chelsea Physic Garden founded in London"],["1899","Aspirin goes on sale"],["2015","Tu Youyou wins Nobel Prize for artemisinin"]],
s2:[["Tu Youyou and artemisinin","In the 1970s, Chinese scientist Tu Youyou searched ancient texts for malaria treatments and found a 4th-century recipe using sweet wormwood soaked in cold water. Her team isolated artemisinin, which has saved millions of lives. She won the Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine in 2015."],["Physic gardens","Medieval monasteries and early universities grew ‘physic gardens’ of medicinal herbs to teach students. The garden founded in Padua, Italy, in 1545 is the oldest university botanical garden still in its original location and is a UNESCO World Heritage Site."]],
sp:["Coriander and genes","Some people think fresh coriander (cilantro) tastes like soap. Studies suggest this is partly genetic: people with certain variations in a smell-receptor gene are more sensitive to soapy-smelling chemicals in the leaves."],
g:[["Herb","A plant used for flavor, medicine or scent; also a non-woody plant"],["Spice","A flavoring from seeds, bark, roots or fruit"],["Aromatic","Having a strong, pleasant smell"],["Physic garden","A garden of medicinal plants"]],
f:["Mint, basil and rosemary are all in the mint family.","Aspirin was developed from a chemical in willow bark.","Some people’s genes make coriander taste like soap."],
fun:"Members of the mint family have square stems, which you can feel by rolling a stem between your fingers."});

ADDART({id:"olives",t:"Olives",c:"Food and Agriculture",i:"🫒",w:"Olive",r:["med","greece","agri","climchange","spaincountry"],
p:[["A Mediterranean tree","The olive tree has been grown for thousands of years around the Mediterranean."],["Fruit and oil","Olives are eaten cured and pressed for olive oil."],["Symbol of peace","The olive branch is a symbol of peace."]],
l:"The olive is a small evergreen tree native to the Mediterranean region, grown for its fruit, which is eaten after curing and pressed to make olive oil. Olive trees have silvery-green leaves, gnarled trunks and can live for many centuries; some trees in the Mediterranean are thought to be more than 2,000 years old. People domesticated olives in the eastern Mediterranean at least 6,000–7,000 years ago, and olive oil became central to ancient Greek, Roman and Middle Eastern life, used for food, lamps, medicine, soap and religious rituals. Fresh olives are extremely bitter and must be cured in water, brine or lye before eating. Spain is by far the largest producer of olive oil, followed by countries including Italy, Greece, Turkey, Tunisia and Morocco. Droughts and heat waves linked to climate change and a deadly bacterial disease have hurt harvests in recent years, sending olive oil prices to record highs in 2023–24.",
s:[["From tree to oil","Olives are harvested in autumn and winter by hand, with rakes or by machines that shake the trees. The fruit is crushed into a paste, mixed and spun to separate the oil. ‘Extra virgin’ olive oil is made without heat or chemicals and has the best flavor; it takes about 5 kilograms of olives to make a liter of oil."],["Green and black olives","Green and black olives can come from the same tree: green olives are picked unripe, while black olives are picked ripe. Some black olives sold in cans are green olives darkened by exposure to air during processing."],["Olives in history and religion","The ancient Greeks believed the goddess Athena gave the olive tree to Athens. Winners at the ancient Olympic Games received olive wreaths. In the Bible, a dove brings Noah an olive leaf, and in Islam the olive is called a blessed tree. Olive oil is used in religious anointing."],["Threats","In 2013, the bacterium Xylella fastidiosa was found in olive trees in Puglia, southern Italy. Spread by insects, it has killed millions of trees, including centuries-old ones. Severe droughts in Spain in 2022 and 2023 roughly halved harvests, driving prices up worldwide before recovering in 2024–25."]],
k:[["Native region","Mediterranean"],["Domesticated","At least 6,000 years ago"],["Largest producer","Spain"],["Oil per liter","About 5 kg of olives"],["Threat","Xylella fastidiosa bacterium"]],
d:[["c. 5000 BC","Olives domesticated in the eastern Mediterranean"],["776 BC","Ancient Olympics begin, with olive wreaths for winners"],["16th century","Spanish colonists take olives to the Americas"],["2013","Xylella disease discovered in Puglia"],["2023–24","Drought pushes olive oil prices to record highs"]],
s2:[["The olive branch","Offering an olive branch has meant making peace since ancient times. Olive branches appear on the flag of the United Nations, surrounding a map of the world, and on the Great Seal of the United States, held by an eagle."],["Ancient trees","Olive trees can regrow from their roots even when the trunk dies, so some are extremely old. The olive tree of Vouves in Crete is estimated to be at least 2,000 years old and still produces olives."]],
sp:["Why fresh olives are bitter","Fresh olives contain a bitter chemical called oleuropein. Curing with water, salt or lye over weeks or months breaks it down. Some olives, such as the Thrubolea of Greece, ripen partly on the tree and are less bitter."],
g:[["Curing","Treating food to preserve it or remove bitterness"],["Extra virgin","The highest grade of olive oil"],["Brine","Salty water used to preserve food"],["Evergreen","A tree that keeps its leaves all year"]],
f:["Green and black olives can grow on the same tree.","Fresh olives are too bitter to eat.","Some olive trees are over 2,000 years old."],
fun:"Ancient Greek athletes rubbed olive oil on their bodies before exercising and scraped it off afterward with a curved tool called a strigil."});

ADDART({id:"grapes",t:"Grapes and Vineyards",c:"Food and Agriculture",i:"🍇",w:"Grape",r:["fruits","vines","agri","francecountry","domestication"],
p:[["Ancient fruit","Grapes were among the first fruits people domesticated."],["Wine, raisins and juice","Grapes are eaten fresh, dried into raisins and made into wine and juice."],["Saved by grafting","An American insect nearly destroyed Europe’s vineyards in the 1800s."]],
l:"Grapes are the berries of woody climbing vines of the genus Vitis, grown in vineyards on every continent except Antarctica. Most cultivated grapes come from the Eurasian grapevine, Vitis vinifera, which a 2023 genetic study suggests was domesticated about 11,000 years ago in western Asia and the Caucasus. Grapes are eaten fresh as table grapes, dried into raisins, sultanas and currants, pressed for juice and fermented into wine. The oldest known evidence of grape wine comes from Georgia, about 8,000 years ago. Grapes need warm, dry summers and grow best in regions with a Mediterranean-type climate. China grows the most grapes, while Italy, France and Spain lead wine production. In the late 19th century, a tiny insect called phylloxera, accidentally brought from North America, destroyed most of Europe’s vineyards; they were saved by grafting European vines onto resistant American roots, which is still done today.",
s:[["The vine","Grapevines climb using tendrils and are trained on wires or posts in vineyards. Growers prune vines heavily each winter. Grapes grow in bunches and change color as they ripen, from green to yellow, red or purple, depending on the variety."],["Thousands of varieties","There are thousands of grape varieties, including Cabernet Sauvignon, Chardonnay and Merlot for wine and Thompson Seedless for eating and raisins. Seedless grapes are produced from natural mutations and grown from cuttings."],["Phylloxera","Phylloxera is a tiny aphid-like insect that feeds on vine roots. It arrived in France in the 1860s and over the next decades destroyed most European vineyards. American vines had evolved resistance, so growers grafted European vines onto American rootstocks, saving the wine industry."],["Climate and grapes","Grapes are sensitive to temperature, and harvest dates in Europe have moved earlier as summers have warmed. Winemakers are planting at higher altitudes and in cooler regions such as southern England and Scandinavia."]],
k:[["Genus","Vitis"],["Main species","Vitis vinifera"],["Domesticated","About 11,000 years ago"],["Oldest wine evidence","Georgia, about 8,000 years ago"],["Largest grape grower","China"]],
d:[["c. 9000 BC","Grapevines domesticated in western Asia"],["c. 6000 BC","Earliest known grape wine in Georgia"],["1863","Phylloxera detected in France"],["1880s","Grafting onto American roots saves European vineyards"],["2023","Genetic study traces grape domestication"]],
s2:[["Raisins","Raisins are grapes dried in the sun or by machines, concentrating their sugar. California and Turkey are major producers. Sultanas are made from green seedless grapes, and currants traditionally from small dark grapes from Greece."],["Grapes and pets","Grapes and raisins can cause sudden kidney failure in dogs, even in small amounts. Scientists believe tartaric acid in the fruit may be responsible. Owners are advised never to feed grapes to dogs."]],
sp:["Ice wine","In Canada and Germany, some grapes are left on the vine until they freeze in winter. Pressing the frozen grapes squeezes out a small amount of very sweet juice, made into a dessert wine called ice wine."],
g:[["Vineyard","A farm or field of grapevines"],["Grafting","Joining part of one plant onto the roots of another"],["Fermentation","Yeast turning sugar into alcohol"],["Rootstock","The root part of a grafted plant"]],
f:["Grapes were domesticated about 11,000 years ago.","The oldest known wine comes from Georgia.","Grapes and raisins are poisonous to dogs."],
fun:"Grapes held near a flame in a microwave oven can spark balls of glowing plasma, an effect scientists explained in a 2019 study, though trying it can damage the oven."});

ADDART({id:"wifi",t:"Wi-Fi",c:"Technology",i:"📶",w:"Wi-Fi",r:["internet","radio","smartphone","smarthome","cybersecurity"],
p:[["Wireless internet","Wi-Fi connects devices to networks and the internet using radio waves."],["A global standard","Wi-Fi follows IEEE 802.11 standards, first published in 1997."],["Everywhere","Billions of devices use Wi-Fi in homes, offices and public places."]],
l:"Wi-Fi is a family of wireless networking technologies that let computers, phones, televisions and other devices connect to a local network and the internet using radio waves instead of cables. A Wi-Fi router connects to the internet through a cable or fiber line and sends and receives data by radio, mostly in frequency bands around 2.4, 5 and 6 gigahertz. Wi-Fi is based on the IEEE 802.11 standards, first published in 1997; the name ‘Wi-Fi’ was created in 1999 by the industry group now called the Wi-Fi Alliance, which certifies devices. Over generations, Wi-Fi has become much faster: the 1999 version reached 11 megabits per second, while Wi-Fi 7, certified from 2024, can in theory exceed 40 gigabits per second. Wi-Fi made laptops and smartphones truly mobile and connects smart home devices. Networks should be protected with strong passwords and encryption, because open networks can let others intercept data.",
s:[["How Wi-Fi works","A router or access point broadcasts a network name. Devices that join exchange data in small packets, encoded onto radio waves. Signals weaken with distance and are blocked by walls and metal, so larger homes may use several access points in a ‘mesh’. The 2.4 GHz band travels farther; the 5 and 6 GHz bands carry more data over shorter distances."],["Origins","In 1971, ALOHAnet in Hawaii linked computers by radio. In 1985, the US government opened certain radio bands for unlicensed use. Researchers in the Netherlands and at Australia’s CSIRO developed key technologies, and the first 802.11 standard appeared in 1997. Apple’s AirPort in 1999 helped bring Wi-Fi to homes."],["Generations","Wi-Fi versions were renamed with simple numbers in 2018: Wi-Fi 4 (802.11n, 2009), Wi-Fi 5 (802.11ac, 2013), Wi-Fi 6 (802.11ax, 2019), Wi-Fi 6E, which added the 6 GHz band, and Wi-Fi 7 (802.11be, certified in 2024). Each generation improved speed and handled more devices at once."],["Security","Early Wi-Fi used WEP encryption, which was easily broken. WPA2, from 2004, and WPA3, from 2018, are much stronger. Public Wi-Fi in cafes and airports may be unencrypted, so experts recommend using secure websites or a virtual private network (VPN) for sensitive information."]],
k:[["Based on","IEEE 802.11 standards"],["First standard","1997"],["Name created","1999"],["Main bands","2.4, 5 and 6 GHz"],["Latest generation","Wi-Fi 7 (certified 2024)"]],
d:[["1971","ALOHAnet links computers by radio in Hawaii"],["1997","First IEEE 802.11 standard"],["1999","The name ‘Wi-Fi’ is introduced"],["2004","WPA2 security introduced"],["2019","Wi-Fi 6 certification begins"],["2024","Wi-Fi 7 certification begins"]],
s2:[["Does Wi-Fi stand for anything?","Wi-Fi is not short for ‘wireless fidelity’, although it was once advertised that way. It was a catchy brand name created by a marketing company, chosen partly to echo ‘hi-fi’ audio equipment."],["Hedy Lamarr","In 1942, actress Hedy Lamarr and composer George Antheil patented a ‘frequency hopping’ system to stop enemies jamming radio-guided torpedoes. Similar spread-spectrum ideas were later used in some early Wi-Fi and in Bluetooth, and Lamarr is often celebrated as a pioneer of wireless technology."]],
sp:["Wi-Fi and health","Wi-Fi uses low-power radio waves that do not have enough energy to damage cells or DNA. The World Health Organization and other health bodies say there is no evidence that Wi-Fi signals at normal levels harm health."],
g:[["Router","A device that directs data between networks"],["Bandwidth","How much data can be sent per second"],["Encryption","Scrambling data so only intended users can read it"],["Gigahertz","Billions of cycles per second"]],
f:["Wi-Fi does not stand for ‘wireless fidelity’.","Wi-Fi standards were first published in 1997.","Walls and metal weaken Wi-Fi signals."],
fun:"Microwave ovens use frequencies close to Wi-Fi’s 2.4 GHz band, which is why a running microwave can sometimes slow down a nearby Wi-Fi connection."});

ADDART({id:"radar",t:"Radar",c:"Technology",i:"📡",w:"Radar",r:["radio","ww2","weatherforecast","airports","microwave","dopplereffect"],
p:[["Seeing with radio waves","Radar finds objects by sending radio waves and timing their echoes."],["Born for war","Radar was developed rapidly before and during World War II."],["Everyday uses","Radar guides planes and ships, forecasts weather and helps cars."]],
l:"Radar, short for ‘radio detection and ranging’, is a system that uses radio waves to detect objects and measure their distance, direction and speed. A radar transmitter sends out pulses of radio waves; when they hit an object such as an aircraft, ship, raindrop or mountain, some energy bounces back to a receiver. Because radio waves travel at the speed of light, the time taken for the echo to return shows how far away the object is, and changes in the echo’s frequency, the Doppler effect, reveal its speed. Early experiments took place in the early 20th century, but radar was developed rapidly in the 1930s by several countries, especially Britain, whose Chain Home network helped detect German aircraft during the Battle of Britain in 1940. Today radar guides air traffic, tracks storms, measures vehicle speeds, helps ships navigate in fog, maps planets and enables driver-assistance systems in cars.",
s:[["How radar works","A radar antenna sends out a narrow beam and then listens for echoes. A display shows each detected object; on older screens, a rotating line sweeps around, leaving bright dots called ‘blips’. Longer radio waves travel farther, while shorter microwaves give more detailed images."],["Radar in World War II","Robert Watson-Watt’s team demonstrated in February 1935 that aircraft could be detected by radio echoes. Britain built the Chain Home stations along its coast by 1939. In 1940, British scientists invented the cavity magnetron, which produced powerful microwaves for compact radar sets in aircraft and ships, and shared it with the United States."],["Weather radar","Weather radars detect raindrops, snow and hail. Doppler weather radar can see winds inside storms, including the rotation that may form tornadoes, giving people more warning time. National weather services operate networks of radar stations."],["Other uses","Police use radar guns to measure car speeds. Ground-penetrating radar helps archaeologists find buried objects. Satellites use radar to map Earth through clouds, and radar from Earth and spacecraft has mapped Venus beneath its thick clouds. Many new cars use small radars for automatic braking and cruise control."]],
k:[["Stands for","Radio detection and ranging"],["Measures","Distance, direction and speed"],["Key wartime system","Chain Home (UK)"],["Key invention","Cavity magnetron (1940)"],["Speed method","Doppler effect"]],
d:[["1904","Christian Hülsmeyer patents a radio ship detector"],["1935","Daventry experiment detects an aircraft by radio echo"],["1939","Chain Home radar network operational"],["1940","Cavity magnetron invented"],["1946","Radar echoes bounced off the Moon"],["1990–94","Magellan spacecraft maps Venus by radar"]],
s2:[["From radar to microwave ovens","In 1945, Percy Spencer, an engineer working on radar magnetrons, noticed that a candy bar in his pocket had melted. He experimented with popcorn and an egg and went on to invent the microwave oven, using the same magnetron technology."],["Stealth","Stealth aircraft are designed to reflect radar waves away from the receiver and absorb some of them with special coatings, making them hard to detect. Engineers develop new types of radar to counter stealth designs."]],
sp:["Bats and dolphins","Bats and dolphins use echolocation, sending out sound waves and listening to echoes, which works on the same principle as radar. Sonar, used by ships to detect submarines and map the seafloor, uses sound instead of radio waves."],
g:[["Echo","A reflected wave"],["Doppler effect","A change in wave frequency caused by motion"],["Magnetron","A device that produces powerful microwaves"],["Sonar","A system that uses sound echoes"]],
f:["Radar stands for radio detection and ranging.","The microwave oven grew out of radar research.","Radar helped Britain in the Battle of Britain."],
fun:"Early radar operators were puzzled by mysterious blips they called ‘angels’, which turned out to be flocks of birds, insects and weather."});

ADDART({id:"jetengine",t:"Jet Engines",c:"Technology",i:"✈️",w:"Jet engine",r:["flight","airports","rocket","steam","ww2"],
p:[["Pushing air backward","Jet engines suck in air, burn fuel and blast hot gas out the back."],["Two inventors","Frank Whittle and Hans von Ohain invented the jet engine separately in the 1930s."],["Shrinking the world","Jet airliners made fast, affordable long-distance travel possible."]],
l:"A jet engine is an engine that produces thrust by pushing out a fast stream of gas, following Isaac Newton’s third law: every action has an equal and opposite reaction. In a typical jet engine, a fan and compressor draw in air and squeeze it, fuel is sprayed in and burned in a combustion chamber, and the hot expanding gases rush out through a turbine, which drives the compressor, and then out of the back at high speed. The jet engine was invented independently in the 1930s by Frank Whittle in Britain and Hans von Ohain in Germany; von Ohain’s engine powered the first jet-powered flight, the Heinkel He 178, on 27 August 1939. Jet fighters appeared late in World War II, and the British de Havilland Comet began the first jet airliner service in 1952. Modern airliners use turbofan engines, which are quieter and more efficient. Aviation produces about 2.5 percent of global carbon dioxide emissions, and engineers are developing cleaner fuels and engines.",
s:[["Types of jet engine","A turbojet pushes all its air through the core and is fast but noisy and thirsty. A turbofan uses a large front fan to push most air around the core, giving more thrust with less fuel and noise; it powers almost all modern airliners. A turboprop uses a jet engine to turn a propeller, and a ramjet has no moving parts but works only at high speeds."],["The inventors","Frank Whittle, a Royal Air Force officer, patented his jet engine design in 1930 but struggled to get funding. Hans von Ohain built his first engine in Germany with support from aircraft maker Ernst Heinkel. Whittle’s engine first flew in the Gloster E.28/39 in May 1941. The two men later met and became friends."],["The jet age","The German Messerschmitt Me 262 became the first operational jet fighter in 1944. The de Havilland Comet began passenger service in May 1952, but crashes caused by metal fatigue grounded it. The Boeing 707, which entered service in 1958, made jet travel common. The Concorde flew passengers at twice the speed of sound from 1976 to 2003."],["Engines today","A large modern turbofan can produce more than 400 kilonewtons of thrust, and its fan can be over 3 meters wide. Engineers are testing sustainable aviation fuels, more efficient open-fan designs and hydrogen-powered engines to reduce emissions."]],
k:[["Principle","Newton’s third law"],["Inventors","Frank Whittle and Hans von Ohain"],["First jet flight","Heinkel He 178, 27 August 1939"],["First jet airliner service","de Havilland Comet, 1952"],["Main modern type","Turbofan"]],
d:[["1930","Frank Whittle patents a jet engine"],["1939","Heinkel He 178 makes the first jet flight"],["1941","Gloster E.28/39 flies with Whittle’s engine"],["1944","Me 262 becomes the first operational jet fighter"],["1952","Comet begins the first jet airliner service"],["1958","Boeing 707 enters service"],["1976","Concorde begins supersonic passenger service"]],
s2:[["Bird strikes","Birds sucked into jet engines can damage them. In January 2009, US Airways Flight 1549 lost both engines after hitting geese and was safely landed on the Hudson River in New York by Captain Chesley Sullenberger, with all 155 people surviving. Engines are tested by firing bird-sized objects into them."],["Why planes leave trails","The white trails behind high-flying jets, called contrails, form when hot, moist exhaust freezes into ice crystals in the cold air. Some contrails spread into thin clouds that trap heat, and airlines are studying ways to avoid creating them."]],
sp:["Hero’s engine","About 2,000 years ago, Hero of Alexandria described the aeolipile, a sphere that spun as steam jets shot out of two bent nozzles. It used the same reaction principle as jet engines but was treated as a curiosity."],
g:[["Thrust","The force that pushes an aircraft forward"],["Turbine","A wheel turned by flowing gas or liquid"],["Compressor","A device that squeezes air"],["Turbofan","A jet engine with a large front fan"]],
f:["The first jet flight was on 27 August 1939.","Most modern airliners use turbofan engines.","Contrails are made of ice crystals."],
fun:"The fan blades of large jet engines spin so fast that their tips can move faster than the speed of sound."});

ADDART({id:"helicopters",t:"Helicopters",c:"Technology",i:"🚁",w:"Helicopter",r:["flight","drones","davinci","marsrovers","firefighters"],
p:[["Rotating wings","Helicopters fly using spinning rotor blades."],["Hover and land anywhere","They can take off vertically, hover and fly in any direction."],["Rescue workers","Helicopters rescue people, fight fires and carry patients."]],
l:"A helicopter is an aircraft that gets lift and thrust from spinning rotor blades, allowing it to take off and land vertically, hover in place and fly forward, backward and sideways. The main rotor’s blades work like spinning wings; tilting them changes the direction of flight. Because the spinning rotor would make the helicopter’s body turn the opposite way, most helicopters have a small tail rotor to counter it, while others use two main rotors spinning in opposite directions. Leonardo da Vinci sketched an ‘aerial screw’ around 1489, but practical helicopters arrived only in the 1930s and 1940s; Igor Sikorsky’s VS-300 first flew in 1939, and his R-4 of 1942 was the first mass-produced helicopter. Helicopters now carry out search and rescue, medical evacuation, firefighting, police and military work, construction and offshore transport. In 2021, NASA’s Ingenuity became the first aircraft to make a powered, controlled flight on another planet, Mars.",
s:[["How a helicopter flies","The pilot uses the collective lever to change the angle of all main blades together, making the helicopter rise or descend, and the cyclic stick to tilt the rotor and move in any direction. Foot pedals change the tail rotor’s thrust to turn the nose. Flying a helicopter requires constant small adjustments."],["Early helicopters","In 1907, the French Breguet and Cornu machines briefly lifted off the ground. The German Focke-Wulf Fw 61, which flew in 1936, was one of the first practical helicopters. Igor Sikorsky’s single-rotor VS-300 design became the model for most later helicopters."],["Helicopters at work","Helicopters were first used to rescue people in the 1940s and to evacuate wounded soldiers in the Korean War. Air ambulances can reach remote accidents quickly. Helicopters drop water on wildfires, lift heavy loads into places without roads and carry workers to oil platforms at sea."],["Ingenuity on Mars","NASA’s Ingenuity, weighing only 1.8 kilograms, traveled to Mars with the Perseverance rover. On 19 April 2021, it made the first powered flight on another planet. Planned for five flights, it made 72 before a damaged blade ended its mission in January 2024."]],
k:[["Lift from","Spinning rotor blades"],["Abilities","Vertical takeoff, hovering, any direction"],["First mass-produced","Sikorsky R-4 (1942)"],["Early sketch","Leonardo da Vinci, c. 1489"],["First flight on Mars","Ingenuity, 19 April 2021"]],
d:[["c. 1489","Leonardo da Vinci sketches an ‘aerial screw’"],["1936","Focke-Wulf Fw 61 flies"],["1939","Sikorsky VS-300 makes its first flight"],["1942","Sikorsky R-4 enters production"],["1946","Bell 47 becomes the first certified civilian helicopter"],["2021","Ingenuity flies on Mars"]],
s2:[["Why a tail rotor?","When an engine spins the main rotor one way, the helicopter’s body tries to spin the other way, an effect called torque. The tail rotor pushes sideways to stop this. Some helicopters, like the Chinook, use two big rotors spinning in opposite directions instead."],["The bamboo-copter","A Chinese toy, the bamboo-copter, a stick with propeller blades spun between the hands, dates back to about 400 BC. Toys like it inspired early aviation pioneers, including the Wright brothers, who played with a rubber-band-powered helicopter as children."]],
sp:["Flying on Mars","Mars’s atmosphere is only about 1 percent as dense as Earth’s, making flight very hard. Ingenuity’s blades were extra long and spun at about 2,500 revolutions per minute, several times faster than a normal helicopter’s, to generate enough lift."],
g:[["Rotor","The spinning blades of a helicopter"],["Hover","To stay in one place in the air"],["Torque","A twisting force"],["Collective and cyclic","The main helicopter flight controls"]],
f:["Leonardo da Vinci sketched a helicopter-like machine around 1489.","Most helicopters need a tail rotor to stop spinning.","A helicopter flew on Mars in 2021."],
fun:"The word helicopter comes from the Greek words helix, ‘spiral’, and pteron, ‘wing’."});

ADDART({id:"washingmachine",t:"Washing Machines",c:"Technology",i:"🧺",w:"Washing machine",r:["elec","feminism","weaving","soap","indrev"],
p:[["Laundry made easy","Washing machines clean clothes with water, detergent and motion."],["Freeing time","They saved people, mostly women, many hours of hard work each week."],["Ever more efficient","Modern machines use much less water and energy than older ones."]],
l:"A washing machine is a household appliance that cleans clothes and other textiles using water, detergent and mechanical action. Before washing machines, laundry was exhausting work done by hand, scrubbing clothes on washboards or beating them on rocks, boiling them in tubs and wringing them out, and could take a whole day each week; this work fell mostly on women and servants. Hand-cranked machines appeared in the 18th and 19th centuries, and electric washing machines were sold in the United States from the early 20th century. Automatic machines that washed, rinsed and spun without help became common after World War II. Today, top-loading machines spin clothes around a vertical drum with an agitator or impeller, while front-loaders tumble clothes in a horizontal drum, using less water. Historians and economists argue that washing machines and other appliances transformed daily life, freeing time for education and paid work.",
s:[["How it works","A washing machine fills with water, adds detergent and moves the clothes to loosen dirt. It then drains the dirty water, rinses the clothes with clean water and spins the drum very fast, flinging water out through holes by centrifugal force. Sensors control water level, temperature and time."],["History","Early patents for washing devices appeared in the 1690s and 1700s. Electric machines were sold from about 1908, including the Thor, though early models had dangerous exposed parts. Bendix introduced an automatic front-loading machine in 1937. By the 1950s and 1960s, washing machines were common in homes in North America and Europe."],["Energy and water","Modern washing machines use far less water and energy than those of a few decades ago. Washing at lower temperatures, such as 30°C, saves energy, and modern detergents work well in cool water. Washing synthetic fabrics releases tiny plastic fibers, and some countries now require filters to catch these microplastics."],["Around the world","Many households still wash clothes by hand, especially in lower-income countries. Public laundries and laundromats serve people without machines. Engineers have designed low-cost, hand-powered machines for homes without electricity."]],
k:[["Main types","Top-loading and front-loading"],["Electric machines sold from","About 1908"],["First automatic","Bendix, 1937"],["Spin cycle","Removes water by centrifugal force"],["Issue","Microplastic fibers"]],
d:[["1797","Washboard patented in the United States"],["c. 1908","Electric washing machines go on sale"],["1937","Bendix introduces an automatic washer"],["1950s","Washing machines become common in Western homes"],["2025","France requires microfiber filters in new machines"]],
s2:[["The washing machine and the internet","Economist Ha-Joon Chang argued in 2010 that the washing machine changed the world more than the internet, because it transformed women’s lives and work. The statistician Hans Rosling told how his grandmother watched the first family washing machine in amazement."],["Laundromats","Coin-operated laundries, called laundromats or launderettes, spread from the 1930s and 1940s. They remain common in cities where apartments lack space for machines and have become community gathering places."]],
sp:["Before washing machines","In many places, laundry day involved carrying water, heating it over a fire, scrubbing with lye soap, rinsing, wringing through rollers called mangles and hanging clothes to dry. A family’s weekly wash could take ten hours or more."],
g:[["Detergent","A chemical that removes dirt and grease"],["Agitator","A post in the drum that moves clothes"],["Centrifugal force","The outward push felt by spinning objects"],["Laundromat","A shop with coin-operated washing machines"]],
f:["Electric washing machines were sold from about 1908.","Front-loaders use less water than top-loaders.","Washing synthetic clothes releases microplastics."],
fun:"Hans Rosling famously called the washing machine ‘the greatest invention of the industrial revolution’, because it freed time for reading and learning."});

ADDART({id:"prosthetics",t:"Prosthetics",c:"Health and the Body",i:"🦾",w:"Prosthesis",r:["olymp","robot","printing3d","nervous","egypt"],
p:[["Replacing body parts","Prostheses are artificial limbs and body parts."],["Ancient history","An artificial toe from ancient Egypt is about 3,000 years old."],["High-tech limbs","Modern prosthetics can be controlled by muscle signals and even give a sense of touch."]],
l:"A prosthesis is an artificial device that replaces a missing body part, such as an arm, leg, hand, foot, eye, tooth or heart valve; the field of designing and fitting them is called prosthetics. People may lose limbs through injury, disease such as diabetes or cancer, or be born without them. Prosthetics have a long history: a wooden and leather toe from ancient Egypt, about 3,000 years old, is one of the oldest known working prostheses. Wars have repeatedly driven improvements, from the iron hands of medieval knights to the lightweight designs developed for wounded soldiers in the 20th and 21st centuries. Modern prosthetic legs include computer-controlled knees and springy carbon-fiber blades used by Paralympic sprinters. Myoelectric arms use sensors to detect muscle signals, letting users open and close a hand by thinking about it, and researchers have connected prostheses to nerves to restore a sense of touch. Cost remains a major barrier: the World Health Organization estimates that only a small share of people who need prosthetic and orthotic devices have access to them.",
s:[["How prosthetic limbs work","A prosthetic limb usually has a socket that fits the remaining limb, a frame and parts such as a knee, ankle or hand. The socket must be carefully shaped to be comfortable. Some prostheses attach directly to the bone through the skin, a technique called osseointegration."],["Historical prostheses","The Cairo toe was found on an Egyptian mummy and shows signs of wear. A Roman bronze-covered leg found in Capua dated to about 300 BC. In the 16th century, French surgeon Ambroise Paré designed hinged legs and hands, and the German knight Götz von Berlichingen used an iron hand with movable fingers."],["Bionic limbs","Myoelectric prostheses were first developed in the Soviet Union in the late 1950s. Today, advanced hands can grip in different ways, and some users can feel pressure through electrodes connected to their nerves. Brain-computer interfaces let some paralyzed people move robotic arms."],["Affordable prosthetics","Many people in lower-income countries cannot afford prostheses. The Jaipur Foot, developed in India in 1968, is a cheap, durable rubber foot that allows squatting and walking barefoot, and has helped more than a million people. Volunteers have used 3D printers to make low-cost hands for children since about 2013."]],
k:[["Definition","An artificial body part"],["Oldest working example","Egyptian toe, about 3,000 years old"],["Muscle-controlled type","Myoelectric prosthesis"],["Affordable design","Jaipur Foot (1968)"],["Sports use","Carbon-fiber running blades"]],
d:[["c. 950–710 BC","Egyptian wooden toe made"],["c. 300 BC","Roman Capua leg made"],["1504","Götz von Berlichingen gets an iron hand"],["1960s","Myoelectric arms come into use"],["1968","Jaipur Foot developed in India"],["2013","Volunteers begin 3D-printing prosthetic hands"]],
s2:[["Running blades","Carbon-fiber running blades, shaped like the letter J, store and release energy like springs. They allow Paralympic athletes to sprint 100 meters in under 11 seconds. Debates have arisen over whether blades could give an advantage over biological legs."],["Not just limbs","Prostheses also include artificial eyes, dental implants, artificial heart valves, hip and knee replacements, and cochlear implants, which help deaf people hear. Breast prostheses are used after cancer surgery."]],
sp:["The Cairo toe","The Cairo toe, made of wood and leather, was found attached to the foot of a mummy of a noblewoman. Tests with volunteers in 2011 showed that replicas helped people walk comfortably in Egyptian-style sandals, suggesting it was a working prosthesis rather than decoration."],
g:[["Prosthesis","An artificial body part"],["Amputation","Removal of a limb"],["Myoelectric","Controlled by muscle electrical signals"],["Socket","The part of a prosthesis that fits the body"]],
f:["The oldest known working prosthesis is an Egyptian toe.","Some prosthetic hands can restore a sense of touch.","The Jaipur Foot has helped over a million people."],
fun:"Pirates in stories often have peg legs and hooks, but real wooden peg legs were common among soldiers and sailors who lost limbs before modern medicine."});

ADDART({id:"motors",t:"Electric Motors",c:"Technology",i:"⚙️",w:"Electric motor",r:["elec","magnet","circuits","currents","ev"],
p:[["Electricity into motion","Electric motors turn electrical energy into movement using magnetism."],["Everywhere","Motors run fans, washing machines, trains, elevators and electric cars."],["A big energy user","Electric motors use almost half of the world’s electricity."]],
l:"An electric motor is a machine that converts electrical energy into mechanical motion, usually rotation. Motors work because an electric current flowing through a wire in a magnetic field experiences a force; by arranging coils of wire and magnets, the force can keep a shaft turning. Michael Faraday demonstrated electromagnetic rotation in 1821, and practical motors followed in the 1830s, but motors spread widely only after power grids were built in the late 19th century. Nikola Tesla and Galileo Ferraris developed the alternating-current induction motor in the 1880s, still the workhorse of industry. Electric motors now power countless machines, from tiny vibration motors in phones to giant motors in ships, trains and factories, as well as fans, pumps, elevators, refrigerators, washing machines, drills and electric cars. The International Energy Agency has estimated that motors and the systems they drive use about 45 percent of the world’s electricity, so making them more efficient saves large amounts of energy.",
s:[["How a motor works","In a simple direct-current motor, a coil of wire sits between magnets. When current flows, the magnetic force pushes one side of the coil up and the other down, making it turn. A device called a commutator reverses the current every half turn so the coil keeps spinning. Generators work the same way in reverse."],["Kinds of motor","DC motors are simple and used in toys and small devices. AC induction motors are rugged and power pumps and factory machines. Brushless DC motors, controlled by electronics, are efficient and used in drones, computer fans and many electric cars. Stepper motors move in precise steps in 3D printers and robots."],["History","Faraday’s 1821 experiment made a wire spin around a magnet. Moritz Jacobi built a practical motor in 1834, and Thomas Davenport patented one in the United States in 1837. Tesla’s 1888 patents for AC motors and the growth of electric power changed factories, which no longer needed a single steam engine driving belts."],["Motors and the future","Electric cars, heat pumps and electric trains all rely on motors. Many powerful motors use magnets made with rare earth elements such as neodymium, mostly mined and processed in China, so engineers are developing designs that need fewer of them."]],
k:[["Converts","Electrical energy to motion"],["Principle","Electromagnetism"],["First demonstration","Michael Faraday, 1821"],["Industrial workhorse","AC induction motor"],["Share of world electricity","About 45% (IEA estimate)"]],
d:[["1821","Faraday demonstrates electromagnetic rotation"],["1834","Moritz Jacobi builds a practical motor"],["1837","Thomas Davenport patents an electric motor"],["1888","Tesla patents the AC induction motor"],["1960s","Brushless DC motors developed"]],
s2:[["Motors in your home","A typical home may contain dozens of electric motors: in the refrigerator, washing machine, vacuum cleaner, fans, hair dryer, blender, computer, phone and electric toothbrush. Many are hidden inside devices."],["Efficiency","Large electric motors can convert more than 95 percent of electrical energy into motion, far more than gasoline engines, which waste most of their energy as heat. Variable-speed drives that adjust motor speed to demand save even more energy."]],
sp:["Faraday’s spinning wire","In September 1821, Michael Faraday hung a wire into a bowl of mercury with a magnet in the middle. When he passed a current through the wire, it circled around the magnet: the first demonstration of electricity producing continuous motion."],
g:[["Electromagnetism","The link between electricity and magnetism"],["Commutator","A switch that reverses current in a motor"],["Induction motor","An AC motor with no brushes"],["Rare earths","A group of metals used in strong magnets"]],
f:["Motors use about 45% of the world’s electricity.","Faraday made the first electric motor in 1821.","A generator is a motor working in reverse."],
fun:"Your phone’s vibration comes from a tiny motor spinning an off-center weight, which shakes the whole phone."});

ADDART({id:"planetdefense",t:"Planetary Defense",c:"Space Exploration",i:"☄️",w:"Planetary defense",r:["comet","asteroidbelt","meteors","dino","scope"],
p:[["Protecting Earth","Planetary defense means finding and stopping dangerous asteroids and comets."],["DART’s success","In 2022, NASA’s DART spacecraft changed an asteroid’s orbit by crashing into it."],["Finding them first","Telescopes search the sky for objects that could hit Earth."]],
l:"Planetary defense is the effort to detect asteroids and comets that might collide with Earth and to develop ways to prevent or prepare for an impact. Large impacts are rare but can be devastating: an asteroid about 10 kilometers wide helped wipe out the dinosaurs 66 million years ago, and a much smaller object flattened about 2,000 square kilometers of forest at Tunguska, Siberia, in 1908. In 2013, a meteor about 20 meters wide exploded over Chelyabinsk, Russia, injuring about 1,500 people, mostly from broken glass. Telescopes around the world have found more than 35,000 near-Earth asteroids, including most of those larger than a kilometer, and none is known to pose a significant threat for at least the next century. On 26 September 2022, NASA’s DART spacecraft deliberately crashed into the small asteroid moon Dimorphos and shortened its orbit by about 33 minutes, the first test of changing an asteroid’s path. The European Space Agency’s Hera spacecraft arrived in November 2026 to study the results.",
s:[["Finding asteroids","Survey telescopes scan the sky each night for moving points of light. Astronomers calculate their orbits and estimate the chance of a future impact. NASA’s NEO Surveyor, an infrared space telescope, is planned for launch around 2027 to find dark asteroids that are hard to see."],["Ways to deflect an asteroid","A spacecraft could crash into an asteroid to nudge it, as DART did. Other ideas include a ‘gravity tractor’, a spacecraft hovering near an asteroid to pull it slowly, or, as a last resort, a nuclear explosion near its surface. The earlier a threat is found, the smaller the nudge needed."],["DART and Hera","DART, about the size of a vending machine with long solar panels, hit Dimorphos at about 6.6 kilometers per second. The impact blasted out a huge cloud of rock and changed the moonlet’s orbit around its larger partner, Didymos, more than expected. Hera, launched in 2024, arrived in November 2026 to measure the crater and the asteroids’ masses."],["Close calls","In December 2024, astronomers found asteroid 2024 YR4, which for a time was calculated to have about a 3 percent chance of hitting Earth in 2032, the highest ever for a large asteroid. Further observations ruled out an Earth impact within weeks. Asteroid Apophis will pass very close to Earth, but safely, on 13 April 2029."]],
k:[["Goal","Detect and deflect dangerous asteroids and comets"],["First deflection test","DART, 26 September 2022"],["DART’s effect","Orbit shortened by about 33 minutes"],["Follow-up mission","ESA’s Hera (arrived November 2026)"],["Next close pass","Apophis, 13 April 2029"]],
d:[["66 million years ago","Asteroid impact helps end the age of dinosaurs"],["1908","Tunguska explosion in Siberia"],["2013","Chelyabinsk meteor injures about 1,500 people"],["2016","NASA sets up its Planetary Defense Coordination Office"],["2022","DART hits Dimorphos"],["2024","Hera launches"],["2026","Hera arrives at Didymos"]],
s2:[["How big is dangerous?","Objects smaller than about 25 meters usually burn up in the atmosphere. Asteroids about 140 meters or larger could destroy a region, and those over 1 kilometer could cause global effects. Astronomers have found most kilometer-sized near-Earth asteroids but still only a fraction of the 140-meter ones."],["Asteroid Day","Asteroid Day, held every year on 30 June, the anniversary of the Tunguska event, raises awareness of asteroid impacts. The United Nations recognized it as International Asteroid Day in 2016."]],
sp:["The Torino scale","Scientists rate the hazard of a possible asteroid impact on the Torino scale from 0 (no hazard) to 10 (certain global catastrophe). Apophis briefly reached level 4 after its discovery in 2004, and 2024 YR4 reached level 3, before both were ruled out as Earth impactors."],
g:[["Near-Earth object","An asteroid or comet whose orbit comes close to Earth’s"],["Deflection","Changing an object’s path"],["Impactor","An object that hits another"],["Kinetic impact","Changing an asteroid’s orbit by hitting it"]],
f:["DART changed an asteroid’s orbit in 2022.","The Chelyabinsk meteor injured about 1,500 people.","Apophis will pass close to Earth in 2029."],
fun:"When Apophis passes Earth in 2029, it will come closer than some satellites and may be visible to the naked eye from parts of the world."});

ADDART({id:"lagrange",t:"Lagrange Points",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"🎯",w:"Lagrange point",r:["grav","jwst","sun","jupiter","asteroidbelt"],
p:[["Parking spots in space","Lagrange points are places where gravity and orbital motion balance."],["Five points","Every pair of large bodies, like the Sun and Earth, has five."],["Home of telescopes","The James Webb Space Telescope orbits the Sun–Earth L2 point."]],
l:"Lagrange points are five special positions in space around two large orbiting bodies, such as the Sun and Earth or Earth and the Moon, where a small object can stay in a fixed position relative to the two. At these points, the gravitational pull of the two bodies combines with the motion of orbiting to balance out. They are named after the Italian-French mathematician Joseph-Louis Lagrange, who described two of them in 1772; Leonhard Euler had found the other three a few years earlier. Three points, L1, L2 and L3, lie on a line through the two bodies and are unstable, so spacecraft there must make small corrections, like balancing a ball on a hilltop. L4 and L5, which lie 60 degrees ahead of and behind the smaller body in its orbit, are stable, and natural objects collect there, such as the thousands of Trojan asteroids that share Jupiter’s orbit. Lagrange points are valuable locations for space telescopes and Sun-watching spacecraft.",
s:[["The five points","For the Sun and Earth, L1 lies about 1.5 million kilometers toward the Sun and L2 about the same distance beyond Earth, away from the Sun. L3 is on the far side of the Sun. L4 and L5 form equilateral triangles with the Sun and Earth."],["Uses of L1 and L2","L1 gives an uninterrupted view of the Sun, so solar observatories such as SOHO, operating since 1996, are stationed there to watch the Sun and warn of space weather. L2 keeps the Sun, Earth and Moon in roughly the same direction, so telescopes like the James Webb Space Telescope, Gaia and Euclid can shield themselves and stay cold."],["Trojan asteroids","Jupiter has thousands of known Trojan asteroids at its L4 and L5 points; the first, 588 Achilles, was found in 1906. NASA’s Lucy spacecraft, launched in 2021, will begin visiting Jupiter Trojans in 2027. Earth has a few small Trojan asteroids, the first, 2010 TK7, found in 2010."],["Halo orbits","Spacecraft do not sit exactly at L1 or L2 but travel in large loops around them, called halo orbits. These keep them out of Earth’s shadow and require only small, occasional engine burns."]],
k:[["Number of points","Five for each pair of bodies"],["Named after","Joseph-Louis Lagrange"],["Stable points","L4 and L5"],["Distance of Sun–Earth L2","About 1.5 million km from Earth"],["Famous resident","James Webb Space Telescope (L2)"]],
d:[["1767","Euler describes L1, L2 and L3"],["1772","Lagrange describes L4 and L5"],["1906","First Jupiter Trojan asteroid found"],["1996","SOHO begins observing the Sun from L1"],["2022","James Webb Space Telescope reaches L2"]],
s2:[["Space dust clouds","Faint clouds of dust, called Kordylewski clouds, may gather at the Earth–Moon L4 and L5 points. Hungarian astronomers reported evidence for them in 2018, but they are very hard to see and still debated."],["A science-fiction favorite","Because L4 and L5 are stable, scientists in the 1970s, led by Gerard O’Neill, proposed building giant space colonies there. The idea inspired science fiction stories and the L5 Society, which promoted space settlement."]],
sp:["Webb’s journey to L2","The James Webb Space Telescope launched on 25 December 2021 and reached its halo orbit around the Sun–Earth L2 point on 24 January 2022, about 1.5 million kilometers from Earth, four times farther than the Moon."],
g:[["Equilibrium","A state where forces balance"],["Halo orbit","A looping orbit around a Lagrange point"],["Trojan asteroid","An asteroid sharing a planet’s orbit at L4 or L5"],["Unstable","Easily pushed away from balance"]],
f:["Every pair of orbiting bodies has five Lagrange points.","Jupiter has thousands of Trojan asteroids.","The James Webb Space Telescope orbits L2."],
fun:"Jupiter’s Trojan asteroids are named after heroes of the Trojan War: Greeks mostly at L4 and Trojans at L5, though a couple of ‘spies’ ended up in the wrong camp."});

ADDART({id:"seasons",t:"The Seasons",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"🍂",w:"Season",r:["stonehenge","sun","climate","calendars","monsoon","autumncolors"],
p:[["Tilted Earth","Seasons happen because Earth’s axis is tilted about 23.4 degrees."],["Not about distance","Earth is actually closest to the Sun in early January."],["Opposite hemispheres","When it is summer in the north, it is winter in the south."]],
l:"The seasons are the yearly cycle of changes in weather, temperature and daylight. They happen because Earth’s axis of rotation is tilted by about 23.4 degrees relative to its orbit around the Sun. As Earth travels around the Sun, the tilt stays pointed in the same direction in space, so for part of the year the Northern Hemisphere leans toward the Sun, receiving more direct sunlight and longer days, which brings summer, while the Southern Hemisphere leans away and has winter. Six months later the situation reverses. A common myth is that seasons are caused by Earth’s changing distance from the Sun; in fact, Earth is closest to the Sun in early January, during northern winter. The solstices, around 21 June and 21 December, mark the longest and shortest days, and the equinoxes, around 20 March and 22 September, have nearly equal day and night everywhere. Tropical regions have little temperature change and often mark wet and dry seasons instead.",
s:[["Why tilt matters","When the Sun is high in the sky, its rays strike the ground more directly, concentrating energy on a smaller area. Long days also give more hours of heating. In winter, the Sun is lower, its light spreads over a larger area, and days are shorter, so less heat is received."],["Solstices and equinoxes","At the June solstice, the Sun is overhead at the Tropic of Cancer, and the Arctic has 24-hour daylight. At the December solstice, it is overhead at the Tropic of Capricorn. At the equinoxes, the Sun is overhead at the equator. Many cultures held festivals at these times, and monuments such as Stonehenge line up with the solstice sunrise or sunset."],["Seasons around the world","Temperate regions have four seasons: spring, summer, autumn and winter. Near the equator, temperatures stay warm all year, and seasons are defined by rainfall, such as monsoon and dry seasons. Polar regions have long, dark winters and summers when the Sun never sets."],["Seasons on other planets","Mars has a tilt similar to Earth’s, giving it seasons about twice as long. Uranus is tilted about 98 degrees, so each pole has about 42 years of continuous sunlight followed by 42 years of darkness. Jupiter, tilted only about 3 degrees, has almost no seasons."]],
k:[["Cause","Earth’s axial tilt of about 23.4°"],["Closest to the Sun","Early January"],["Solstices","About 21 June and 21 December"],["Equinoxes","About 20 March and 22 September"],["Most extreme tilt","Uranus, about 98°"]],
d:[["c. 3000–2500 BC","Stonehenge built, aligned with solstice sunrise and sunset"],["c. 130 BC","Hipparchus measures the timing of the seasons"],["1543","Copernicus explains seasons with a moving Earth"],["1582","Gregorian calendar keeps equinox near 21 March"]],
s2:[["Astronomical and meteorological seasons","Astronomers start the seasons at the solstices and equinoxes, so northern spring begins around 20 March. Weather scientists use whole months instead: in the Northern Hemisphere, spring is March to May, summer June to August, autumn September to November and winter December to February."],["Seasons and living things","Plants and animals respond to changing day length and temperature. Trees drop leaves in autumn, birds migrate, and some mammals hibernate. Climate change is shifting the timing of seasons, with spring arriving earlier in many regions."]],
sp:["The midnight sun","North of the Arctic Circle and south of the Antarctic Circle, the Sun stays above the horizon all day for at least one day in summer, and at the poles for about six months. In winter, these regions have a ‘polar night’ when the Sun does not rise."],
g:[["Axis","An imaginary line around which a planet spins"],["Solstice","The day with the longest or shortest daylight"],["Equinox","When day and night are about equal"],["Hemisphere","Half of the Earth"]],
f:["Seasons are caused by Earth’s tilt, not its distance from the Sun.","Earth is closest to the Sun in January.","Uranus has 42-year seasons."],
fun:"Because Earth moves faster when closer to the Sun, northern winter is about five days shorter than northern summer."});

ADDART({id:"spacewalks",t:"Spacewalks",c:"Space Exploration",i:"👩‍🚀",w:"Extravehicular activity",r:["astronauts","iss","gagarin","hubblest","commspace"],
p:[["Working outside","A spacewalk is when an astronaut leaves a spacecraft to work in space."],["First in 1965","Alexei Leonov made the first spacewalk on 18 March 1965."],["Building the ISS","Hundreds of spacewalks built and maintain the International Space Station."]],
l:"A spacewalk, or extravehicular activity (EVA), is any activity done by an astronaut outside a spacecraft in space or on the surface of another world. Spacewalkers wear pressurized spacesuits that provide oxygen, remove carbon dioxide, control temperature and protect against radiation and tiny meteoroids; each suit is effectively a small personal spacecraft. Soviet cosmonaut Alexei Leonov made the first spacewalk on 18 March 1965, and American Ed White followed in June 1965. Spacewalks have been used to repair satellites, service the Hubble Space Telescope, build and maintain space stations and explore the Moon. More than 270 spacewalks have been carried out at the International Space Station. In 2024, Jared Isaacman and Sarah Gillis made the first commercial spacewalk during the private Polaris Dawn mission. The longest spacewalk, by Chinese astronauts Cai Xuzhe and Song Lingdong in December 2024, lasted 9 hours and 6 minutes.",
s:[["The spacesuit","A spacewalking suit has many layers, a helmet with a sun visor, gloves and a backpack containing oxygen, batteries, water for cooling and radios. Suits are pressurized, which makes them stiff; moving the hands in gloves is especially tiring. Astronauts breathe pure oxygen for hours beforehand to avoid decompression sickness."],["Staying safe","Spacewalkers are tethered to the station and move hand over hand along rails. NASA astronauts wear a small jet backpack called SAFER for emergencies. Spacewalks are usually done in pairs and practiced for months in giant swimming pools, where floating underwater resembles weightlessness."],["Famous spacewalks","Leonov’s suit ballooned in the vacuum, and he had to let out air to squeeze back inside. In 1984, Bruce McCandless made the first untethered spacewalk using a jet backpack, and Svetlana Savitskaya became the first woman to walk in space. Hubble repair spacewalks from 1993 to 2009 kept the telescope working. In 2019, Christina Koch and Jessica Meir made the first all-female spacewalk."],["Dangers","In 2013, Italian astronaut Luca Parmitano’s helmet began filling with water during a spacewalk, and he returned safely despite being nearly blinded. Spacewalkers face extreme temperatures, from about 120°C in sunlight to −150°C in shadow, as well as sharp edges and debris."]],
k:[["Also called","Extravehicular activity (EVA)"],["First spacewalk","Alexei Leonov, 18 March 1965"],["First woman","Svetlana Savitskaya, 1984"],["First commercial","Polaris Dawn, 12 September 2024"],["Longest","9 h 6 min (December 2024)"]],
d:[["1965","Leonov makes the first spacewalk"],["1969","First moonwalk, by Neil Armstrong and Buzz Aldrin"],["1984","First untethered spacewalk; first woman spacewalker"],["1993","First Hubble servicing mission"],["2019","First all-female spacewalk"],["2024","First commercial spacewalk; longest spacewalk"]],
s2:[["Records","Russian cosmonaut Anatoly Solovyev holds the record for the most total time spacewalking, about 82 hours over 16 spacewalks. NASA’s Peggy Whitson and Sunita Williams have each spent more than 60 hours outside, the most of any women."],["Lost in space?","Astronauts have occasionally lost tools during spacewalks. In 2008, Heidemarie Stefanyshyn-Piper lost a tool bag that orbited Earth for months and could be seen from the ground with binoculars before burning up in the atmosphere."]],
sp:["Training underwater","NASA’s Neutral Buoyancy Laboratory in Houston is a pool 62 meters long and 12 meters deep containing full-size models of parts of the space station. Astronauts train there in spacesuits for about seven hours for every hour they will spend spacewalking."],
g:[["EVA","Extravehicular activity, a spacewalk"],["Tether","A safety line"],["Pressurized","Filled with air at a set pressure"],["Decompression sickness","Illness caused by gas bubbles forming in the body"]],
f:["The first spacewalk was in 1965.","The first commercial spacewalk was in 2024.","Astronauts train for spacewalks in giant pools."],
fun:"After his spacewalk, Alexei Leonov became an artist and painted some of the first artworks made in space, using colored pencils aboard Voskhod 2."});

ADDART({id:"spaceports",t:"Spaceports",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🛫",w:"Spaceport",r:["rocket","commspace","sputnik","gagarin","apollo"],
p:[["Where rockets launch","Spaceports are sites for launching and sometimes landing spacecraft."],["Near the equator","Launch sites near the equator give rockets a speed boost from Earth’s spin."],["A growing number","New spaceports are being built around the world for commercial launches."]],
l:"A spaceport, or cosmodrome, is a site for launching and sometimes landing spacecraft. Spaceports include launch pads, assembly buildings, fuel storage, control centers and tracking stations. They are usually built near coasts or in remote areas so that rockets fly over water or empty land, reducing danger if something goes wrong. Sites close to the equator give rockets a free boost from Earth’s rotation, which is fastest there, and are good for launching into orbits above the equator. The world’s first and largest spaceport, Baikonur Cosmodrome in Kazakhstan, launched Sputnik 1 in 1957 and Yuri Gagarin in 1961. Other major spaceports include the Kennedy Space Center and Cape Canaveral in Florida, from which the Apollo missions launched; Europe’s Guiana Space Centre in French Guiana, close to the equator; China’s Jiuquan and Wenchang centers; India’s Satish Dhawan Space Centre; and Japan’s Tanegashima. Private companies such as SpaceX now run their own launch sites, including Starbase in Texas.",
s:[["Choosing a site","Spaceports need safe areas downrange where rockets and falling stages will not harm people. East-facing coasts are ideal because rockets usually launch eastward to use Earth’s spin. Polar orbits are reached by launching north or south, as from Vandenberg in California."],["Baikonur","Built by the Soviet Union in the 1950s on the steppes of Kazakhstan, Baikonur launched the first satellite, the first human in space and the first space station. Russia leases it from Kazakhstan, and Soyuz rockets still carry astronauts from there to the International Space Station."],["Cape Canaveral and Kennedy","Florida’s Space Coast hosted the Mercury, Gemini and Apollo programs and the Space Shuttle. Launch Complex 39A sent Apollo 11 to the Moon and is now used by SpaceX. The area has become one of the busiest launch sites in the world."],["New spaceports","Countries including the United Kingdom, Norway, Sweden, Australia and South Korea have built or are building spaceports for small rockets. SpaceX’s Starbase in southern Texas, where Starship is tested, became an official city in 2025. Floating platforms and drone ships at sea are also used for launching and landing rockets."]],
k:[["Also called","Cosmodrome"],["First and largest","Baikonur Cosmodrome, Kazakhstan"],["Apollo launch site","Kennedy Space Center, Florida"],["Europe’s spaceport","Guiana Space Centre, Kourou"],["Equator advantage","Boost from Earth’s rotation"]],
d:[["1955","Baikonur Cosmodrome founded"],["1957","Sputnik 1 launched from Baikonur"],["1961","Gagarin launches from Baikonur"],["1968","First launch from Kourou, French Guiana"],["1969","Apollo 11 launches from Launch Complex 39A"],["2025","Starbase in Texas becomes a city"]],
s2:[["Why so close to the equator?","Earth’s surface at the equator moves eastward at about 1,670 kilometers per hour. A rocket launched eastward there starts with this speed, saving fuel. Kourou, at about 5 degrees north, has an advantage over Baikonur, at about 46 degrees north."],["Gagarin’s Start","The pad at Baikonur from which Yuri Gagarin launched in 1961, known as Gagarin’s Start, was used for crewed flights for decades. Cosmonauts follow traditions there, such as planting trees and watching a particular film before launch."]],
sp:["Launching from the sea","Rockets have also been launched from ships and floating platforms, which can move to the equator. SpaceX lands rocket boosters on autonomous drone ships at sea so they can be reused."],
g:[["Spaceport","A site for launching spacecraft"],["Launch pad","The platform a rocket launches from"],["Downrange","The area a rocket flies over after launch"],["Cosmodrome","A Russian term for spaceport"]],
f:["Baikonur is the world’s first and largest spaceport.","Rockets launch eastward to use Earth’s spin.","Starbase in Texas became a city in 2025."],
fun:"Cosmonauts launching from Baikonur traditionally stop on the way to the pad to relieve themselves on the bus’s rear wheel, as Yuri Gagarin is said to have done in 1961."});

ADDART({id:"solarsails",t:"Solar Sails",c:"Space Exploration",i:"⛵",w:"Solar sail",r:["sun","light","rocket","sailing","sat"],
p:[["Sailing on sunlight","Solar sails push spacecraft using the pressure of sunlight."],["No fuel needed","They can keep accelerating as long as sunlight shines on them."],["Tested in space","Japan’s IKAROS first flew a solar sail between planets in 2010."]],
l:"A solar sail is a method of propelling a spacecraft using the tiny pressure of sunlight on a large, thin, mirror-like sheet. Light carries momentum, so when particles of light, called photons, bounce off a sail, they give it a very gentle push. The force is extremely small, but in the vacuum of space there is no air resistance, and a sail never runs out of fuel, so the spacecraft can gradually build up great speed. Sails are made of plastic films only a few micrometers thick, coated with aluminum, and must be very large and light. The idea was proposed in the early 20th century by scientists including Konstantin Tsiolkovsky and Friedrich Zander. In 2010, Japan’s IKAROS became the first spacecraft to use a solar sail in interplanetary space, and The Planetary Society’s LightSail 2 showed controlled solar sailing in Earth orbit in 2019. NASA’s Advanced Composite Solar Sail System tested lightweight booms in 2024.",
s:[["How a sail works","When sunlight reflects from the sail, it pushes the sail away from the Sun. By tilting the sail, like a sailboat, the spacecraft can direct the force to speed up or slow down in its orbit, moving outward or inward through the solar system. The push near Earth is about the weight of a sheet of paper on a large sail."],["Missions","Japan’s IKAROS, launched in 2010 with the Akatsuki Venus probe, unfurled a 14-meter-wide sail and steered by changing the brightness of panels on its edges. NASA’s NanoSail-D2 flew in 2011, and LightSail 2 raised its orbit using sunlight in 2019. NASA’s ACS3, launched in April 2024, deployed an 80-square-meter sail on composite booms."],["Future uses","Solar sails could hover in unusual positions to watch the Sun, carry cargo slowly through the solar system or visit asteroids without fuel. Breakthrough Starshot, announced in 2016, proposed using powerful lasers on Earth to push tiny sails to 20 percent of the speed of light toward Alpha Centauri, though it faces enormous technical challenges."],["Challenges","Sails must be extremely large, light and strong, and packed into small spacecraft before unfolding in space. Deploying them without tears or tangles is difficult, and their push is weak near the outer planets, where sunlight is faint."]],
k:[["Propulsion","Pressure of sunlight"],["Material","Thin aluminum-coated plastic"],["First interplanetary sail","IKAROS (Japan, 2010)"],["Orbit-raising demonstration","LightSail 2 (2019)"],["Fuel needed","None"]],
d:[["1920s","Tsiolkovsky and Zander propose sailing on sunlight"],["2010","IKAROS unfurls its sail between Earth and Venus"],["2016","Breakthrough Starshot announced"],["2019","LightSail 2 raises its orbit using sunlight"],["2024","NASA’s ACS3 deploys its sail"]],
s2:[["Kepler’s idea","In 1608, Johannes Kepler noticed that comet tails point away from the Sun and suggested that a ‘solar breeze’ pushed them. He wrote to Galileo that ships could one day sail through space with sails adapted to this breeze."],["Sails and science fiction","Solar sails appear in many science fiction stories, including Arthur C. Clarke’s ‘The Wind from the Sun’ (1964), about a solar yacht race. Space sailing remains a popular image of gentle, fuel-free travel."]],
sp:["IKAROS","The name IKAROS stands for Interplanetary Kite-craft Accelerated by Radiation Of the Sun, a reference to Icarus of Greek myth, who flew too close to the Sun. Its sail was thinner than a human hair and also carried thin-film solar cells to generate power."],
g:[["Photon","A particle of light"],["Momentum","The quantity of motion an object has"],["Propulsion","The force that moves a vehicle"],["Boom","A long pole that holds a sail open"]],
f:["Sunlight can push a spacecraft.","Solar sails need no fuel.","IKAROS was the first interplanetary solar sail."],
fun:"Johannes Kepler imagined sailing to the stars in 1608, more than 400 years before the first solar sail flew."});
